---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { atlas, shortSha } from "../lib/atlas";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const commit = atlas.upstream.commit;
---

<Base
  title="About · Math Release Atlas"
  description="What this unofficial atlas is, how Lean status should be read, and the Apache-2.0 notice for derived catalogue text."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      Math Release Atlas is an unofficial index of three public catalogues. One is the result families
      in the manuscript collection at <a href="https://github.com/openai/math">github.com/openai/math</a>.
      Another is the Lean files in{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results">github.com/google-deepmind/alphaproof-nexus-results</a>,
      read with the paper at <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22763">arXiv 2605.22763</a>.
      The third is the Anthropic repositories{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/formal-math">github.com/anthropics/formal-math</a>{" "}
      and{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem">github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem</a>.
      It is not affiliated with OpenAI, Google DeepMind, or Anthropic.
    </p>
    <p>
      The upstream collection groups manuscripts into families. This site turns that catalogue into
      a table: subject, manuscript dates, PDF links, and whether Lean records a formalized main
      result, only a Comparator challenge, or neither. Each row links to a family page. Lens pages
      list the families under each hand-written tag. A family with no tag still shows community
      status claimed. The commit pinned in the data is{" "}
      <a href={`${atlas.upstream.repo}/commit/${commit}`}><code>{shortSha(commit)}</code></a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Review status</h2>
    <p>
      On this pinned commit, the upstream formalization catalogue sets its review status to{" "}
      <strong>{atlas.upstream.review_status}</strong>. Every Lean badge on the table repeats that
      status. This atlas did not run those checks.
    </p>
    <p>
      A Lean-checked manuscript is not a peer-reviewed paper and is not a laboratory result. Text
      marked “Upstream summary” is the catalogue’s own wording, quoted here.
    </p>

    <h2>Citations</h2>
    <p>
      Family pages list the other families cited from upstream manuscript sources. Each edge comes
      from an <code>OAI:</code> key and links to the file that contains that key. The{" "}
      <a href={`${base}graph/`}>citation graph</a> draws those edges and repeats them in a list.
      A shared-topic link, when one is recorded, is labeled our grouping, not a citation.
    </p>

    <h2>Community status</h2>
    <p>
      Every family in this catalogue is claimed until a pull request says otherwise. A status other
      than claimed needs an evidence URL, a date, and a short note. The{" "}
      <a href={`${base}status/`}>status page</a> lists the vocabulary. Every status change must be
      approved by Jason (repo policy).
      The daily sync does not set a status.
    </p>

    <h2>Cautions that always appear</h2>
    <p>These notes belong to the atlas. They are not upstream claims.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={`${base}f/002/`}>002</a>. BSD for low Selmer corank — not full BSD.</li>
      <li>
        <a href={`${base}f/003/`}>003</a>. Zero-free region Re s &gt; 7/8 ('quasi-Riemann hypothesis')
        — not the Riemann Hypothesis.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={`${base}f/032/`}>032</a>. Hodge for CM abelian varieties — not the full Hodge
        conjecture.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={`${base}f/102/`}>102</a>, <a href={`${base}f/103/`}>103</a>, and{" "}
        <a href={`${base}f/107/`}>107</a>. Very large claims (UGC, L=RL, ω ≤ 9/4); treat as claims
        until checked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={`${base}f/376/`}>376</a>. Forced Navier–Stokes computation — not the Clay Millennium
        Navier–Stokes problem.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>AlphaProof Nexus</h2>
    <p>
      The second source is pinned at its own commit. Each row is one Lean file from that commit.
      A natural-language PDF is linked only when the filename corresponds. The source page records
      the paper count and the repository count side by side. Per-row provenance is MISSING.
      Lean file deposited upstream; upstream CI builds it. This atlas did not run Lean.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={`${base}source/alphaproof-nexus/`}>AlphaProof Nexus source page</a>
      {" · "}
      <a href={`${base}source/cross/`}>Cross-source problem ids</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Anthropic</h2>
    <p>
      The third source is two repositories at their own commits. Each release is one row.
      Fermat's Last Theorem is a formalization of a known theorem, not a new result.
      Real-number inputs and some running-time claims in the 3SUM and APSP paper are not formalized.
      The zeta release is a lower bound on the proportion of zeta zeros that are simple and on the critical line (more than two thirds). It is not the Riemann Hypothesis.
      Lean files deposited upstream. This atlas did not run Lean. Both repositories are Apache-2.0.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={`${base}source/anthropic/`}>Anthropic source page</a>
    </p>

    <h2>License</h2>
    <p>
      The atlas is released under Apache-2.0. The OpenAI Math collection is also Apache-2.0. A copy of
      that license ships in this repository as <code>UPSTREAM_LICENSE</code>.
      AlphaProof Nexus code is Apache-2.0. Its other materials are CC-BY 4.0. OEIS-derived material
      is CC BY-SA 4.0. The Anthropic repositories linked here are Apache-2.0. See <code>NOTICE</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      We changed and derived these files. Titles and summaries from the upstream catalogue were
      reformatted into JSON and into this website. The originals, including the PDFs, stay in the
      upstream repository.
    </p>

    <h2>What is generated</h2>
    <p>
      A script reads one upstream commit and writes <code>data/upstream.json</code>. It does not
      edit <code>data/curated/</code>. A merge step combines the two. A curated lens or related-work
      note has to carry a source, or the build fails. The upstream commit stored on each family is
      the provenance for the generated fields.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
